{
  "image_name": "3dbc8588ecbabe6644d89ff110fb5c42.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways plasmonic metal nanostructures can drive charge or energy transfer to nearby semiconductors. At the top, a CdSe nanorod capped with Au is shown with an energy-level sketch. Visible light excites electrons across the semiconductor band gap, while infrared excitation involves higher electron states and coupling to a localized surface plasmon near the Au tip. The drawing suggests electron transfer from the nanorod toward the metal/metallic states around the Fermi level, mediated by the plasmon.\n\nThe middle schematic compares photoinduced carrier pathways at an Au/Ti/TiO₂ junction. Illumination generates both interband electrons from the Au d-band and plasmonic hot electrons. The total photocurrent is indicated as the sum of plasmonic and interband contributions. Energetic electrons are injected into TiO₂ conduction-band states, while holes remain in or move through the metal side, emphasizing charge separation across the interface.\n\nThe bottom panel shifts from charge transfer to optical energy transfer. It shows a donor plasmon resonance overlapping with acceptor absorption bands, illustrating two regimes: PIRET and FRET. The key message is that spectral overlap controls whether plasmonic structures enhance semiconductor excitation by direct hot-carrier injection or by near-field resonant energy transfer.",
